## Authentication

Every route under `/api/v1` needs `Authorization: Bearer <token>`; `/health`{{if .Observability}} and `/metrics`{{end}} stay public.
Tokens are HS256 JWTs with `sub` and `exp`, signed with `JWT_SECRET` by your identity service.
`go run ./cmd/token -sub alice` signs one for development. Handlers read the caller with
`auth.FromContext(ctx)`.
{{- end}}
{{- if .Observability}}

## Metrics and tracing

`GET /metrics` serves `http_request_duration_seconds{method,route,status}` plus Go runtime metrics
for Prometheus. Every request gets an OpenTelemetry span that continues an incoming `traceparent`;
spans are exported when `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT` is set, and request logs carry `trace_id`
and `span_id`.
{{- end}}
{{- if .Redis}}

## Cache

`{{.P.cache.Dir}}` stores JSON values in Redis with a TTL (`Get`, `Set`, `Delete`); keys are
prefixed with the service name. `/health` pings Redis too. Test against a real Redis with
`TEST_REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379/15 go test ./...`.
{{- end}}
{{- if .Migrations}}

## Migrations

SQL files in `migrations/` (`<version>_<name>.up.sql` / `.down.sql`) are embedded into the binary
and applied with golang-migrate when the service starts. Versions run in numeric order and
applied ones are recorded in the `schema_migrations` table.
`projgen add entity` writes its table's migration here, so it runs on the next start.

Test them against a throwaway database:

```bash
TEST_DATABASE_URL=... go test -run 'Migrate|Repository' ./...
```
{{- end}}

## Logging

Logs are JSON with snake_case fields: `@timestamp`, `level`, `message`, `service`, `env`, `request_id`, `logger`.
Keep `message` constant and put dynamic values in their own fields:

```go
logger.FromContext(ctx, log).Info("order created", "order_id", id)
```

Never log passwords, tokens, API keys or personal data.
